### BGP-4 Specification Update (RFC4271bis)
* **Presenter:** John Scudder
* **Draft:** `draft-ietf-idr-bgp4-rfc4271bis`
* **Slides:** [RFC4271bis Update](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-idr-sessa-1-1-rfc4271bis-update-01)
* **Discussion:**
  * John Scudder summarized updates through versions -04, -05, and -06.
  * Key updates include: integrating RFC 7606 error handling, AS 0 processing, Extended Optional Parameters, deprecation of `AS_SET` and `AS_CONFED_SET`, correcting the AGGREGATOR attribute so it is not restricted to IPv4, removing outdated "well-known" terminology in favor of explicit attribute bit flags, and integrating RFC 8212 into path selection.
  * Version -06 introduced an "omnibus" integration containing BGP Capabilities and 4-byte AS support into the main specification text, moving 2-byte AS operations to a backward-compatibility section.
  * Formal polls confirmed consensus to proceed with the omnibus approach and to express guidance preferring MP-BGP over legacy IPv4 NLRI encodings.
  * John Scudder noted ongoing work to integrate RFC 4760 (MP-BGP) and RFC 2545 into upcoming revisions (-07). Discussions confirmed that parsing NLRI encoding relies on both AFI and SAFI.

### BGP over TLS/TCP
* **Presenter:** Serge Maskit
* **Slides:** [BGP over TLS/TCP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-idr-sessa-1-2-bgp-over-tlstcp-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Serge Maskit presented updates in version -05. The draft reverted to standard TCP port 179 using implicit TLS rather than requesting a dedicated port.
  * Active peers initiate sessions with a TLS ClientHello before any plain-text BGP exchange, preserving the standard BGP Finite State Machine (FSM).
  * The draft mandates TCP-AO protection prior to TLS and mandates TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication. An Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) token (`BOTLS`) is defined.
  * The document status was moved from Experimental to Standards Track due to vendor interest.
  * Sriram Parameswaran asked about vendor implementations; Cisco expressed implementation interest. Alvaro Retana, Ketan Talaulikar, and John Scudder discussed mandatory TCP-AO requirements and their alignment with `draft-ietf-idr-bgp4-rfc4271bis`.

### BGP Signaling of MASQUE Tunnel Encapsulation
* **Presenter:** Alvaro Retana
* **Slides:** [BGP Signaling of MASQUE Tunnel Encapsulation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-idr-sessa-1-3-bgp-signaling-of-masque-tunnel-encapsulation-00)
* **Discussion:**
  * Alvaro Retana presented updates on signaling MASQUE tunnels using the BGP Tunnel Encapsulation Attribute (RFC 9012).
  * The draft defines MASQUE tunnel types (Connect TCP, Connect UDP, Connect IP, Connect Ethernet) and introduces two sub-TLVs: URI Template and Service Parameters (modeled after DNS SVCB records).
  * Rather than using RFC 9012 Tunnel Egress Endpoint sub-TLVs, validation uses IP literals in the URI or IPv4/IPv6 hints from the Service Parameters sub-TLV.
  * Susan Hares raised concerns regarding text describing validation procedures versus RFC 9012. Alvaro agreed to update the text to clarify the validation mechanism explicitly.

